00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
it's a standoff between the world's most  prestigious tennis tournament and the  sport's global governing bodies both the  atp and wta tours have stripped  wimbledon of ranking points meaning  players won't be able to earn any here  this year because the tournament banned  players from russia and belarus woodward  and says it had no option but to exclude  them under uk government guidance  following russia's invasion of ukraine  in a statement put out by the club on  friday it called the decision not to  award points disproportionate in the  context of the exceptional and extreme  circumstances going on to say that it  was damaging to all players who compete  on the tour  the atp and wta have allowed players  from russia and belarus to compete as  neutrals including at the paris open  starting on sunday  the atp has put out a statement  explaining why it was effectively  turning wimbledon into an exhibition  tournament  it says the ability for players of any  nationality to enter tournaments based  on merit and without discrimination is  fundamental to our tour  wimbledon's ban ruled out top players  including men's world number two daniel  medvedev from russia and last year's  women's semi-finalist irina sabalenko  from belarus  serbia's novak djokovic is one of  several players to have criticized the  ban now he's set to lose his world  number one status as he can't get the  2000 points he earned by winning here  last year but experts don't predict a  boycott it's really an interesting sort  of play of power and we'll see sort of  who has the strength and the leverage  here but i think wimbledon feels as  though they are the most prestigious  event you'll notice that no players are  boycotting it's not until the players  who they might be angry but they're not  angry enough to stay home the all  england lawn tennis club says it's  considering its options and is in  discussions with its grand slam  colleagues but the reverberations from  its decision are likely to be felt for  some time to come nadim baba al jazeera  london
 

